<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ausgleich, Compromise of 1867, 1867 Compromise</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867, or Ausgleich, was the agreement between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> that created the dual monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. Hungary received its own parliament and government, while foreign affairs, the army and joint finances were shared. It founded the Austro-Hungarian Empire and shaped its governance until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867.jpg" alt="Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion / modified by AndreasPraefcke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cisleithanien_Transleithanien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1526 to 1804 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as kings, but the kingdom remained nominally and legally separate from the other Habsburg lands. Unlike them it had an old historic constitution that limited the power of the Crown. In March 1848 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-diet">Hungarian Diet</a> approved the April Laws, which abolished the privileges of the nobility, and King Ferdinand signed them on 11 April 1848. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> later revoked them without legal authority, and an absolutist administration followed.</p><p>The suppression of the 1848 revolutions, the mobilisation of the Crimean War and a disastrous campaign against Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1859 brought the state to the verge of bankruptcy, and in 1866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> were forced to reconcile with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to save their empire and dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> were reorganised as a real union between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> under a single monarch, who reigned as Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in one half and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in the other. Each half had its own parliament and prime minister. The old Hungarian constitution, legal system and judiciary were restored, and the monarch accepted the April Laws except those based on the 9th and 10th points.</p><p>Common ministries of foreign affairs and defence were kept under the monarch, with a third finance ministry responsible only for financing those two. There was no common parliament, only delegations of 60 members from each parliament, whose common decisions had to be ratified by both. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 8 June 1867 in Matthias Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many Hungarians saw the settlement as a betrayal of the 1848 reforms. In his Cassandra Letter of 22 May 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> wrote: &quot;I see in this act the death of the nation.&quot; Democratic circles spread across the Great Hungarian Plain, and the counties of Heves, Pest and Szatmár rejected the settlement. Fewer than 8 per cent of the population had the vote when it was enacted. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> it was unpopular too, widely regarded as a forced concession, and the Austrian Parliament was presented with a fait accompli.</p><p>Ethnic Hungarian voters generally did not support the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>, which kept its majority through the votes of ethnic minorities. The favouring of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> caused discontent among Slovaks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Compromise of 1867</b> recognised the Kingdom of Hungary as an equal partner with its own government and parliament, joined to the remaining lands by the person of the monarch and by common ministries for foreign affairs, war and joint finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ausgleich</b>, or Compromise, of 1867 was negotiated between Emperor Franz Joseph I and Hungarian liberal leaders, above all Ferenc Deák and Count Gyula Andrássy, who recognised that a weakened Austrian Empire needed Hungarian cooperation and that Hungary could extract far-reaching autonomy from a dynasty in crisis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the revolutions Russia abolished serfdom in 1861, France kept the universal male suffrage the Second Republic had set up, and the Austrian Empire was reorganised into the Dual Monarchy through the <b>Ausgleich</b> of 1867, spearheaded by the former revolutionaries Gyula Andrássy and Ferenc Deák.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1849 Transylvania was again a separate crown land, until the <b>Compromise of 1867</b> merged it into the Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>Neo-absolutist rule followed until the <b>Compromise of 1867</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat by Prussia in 1866 forced the <b>Compromise of 1867</b>, which created Austria-Hungary and the self-governing Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1526-1867">Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)</a></li></ul></section>
